1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,666
You wanted to be rich.

2
00:00:01,666 --> 00:00:04,927
A silk shirt, a sword with a patterned blade,

3
00:00:04,927 --> 00:00:06,623
and a pouch of silver coins covered

4
00:00:06,623 --> 00:00:09,537
in writing that nobody at home can read.

5
00:00:09,537 --> 00:00:11,812
Instead, it is barely light,

6
00:00:11,812 --> 00:00:14,795
you are up to your waist in a freezing river,

7
00:00:14,795 --> 00:00:16,682
and you are pushing a boat over rocks

8
00:00:16,682 --> 00:00:19,485
while somebody shouts the name of the rapid.

9
00:00:19,485 --> 00:00:20,924
It is called Do not sleep.

10
00:00:20,924 --> 00:00:23,150
Somebody has to push the boat.

11
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:25,141
Today, that somebody is you.

12
00:00:25,611 --> 00:00:26,909
Imagine you are Asmund,

13
00:00:26,909 --> 00:00:30,423
the younger brother of Aslaug from our earlier chapters.

14
00:00:30,423 --> 00:00:32,011
Like Eirik and Aslaug,

15
00:00:32,011 --> 00:00:34,019
you are an imagined person,

16
00:00:34,019 --> 00:00:36,958
built from what archaeology and written sources tell us,

17
00:00:36,958 --> 00:00:38,879
not a real man from history.

18
00:00:38,879 --> 00:00:41,304
You are a lean young man with fair

19
00:00:41,304 --> 00:00:44,295
hair tied back, a short fair beard,

20
00:00:44,295 --> 00:00:46,598
a rust-orange tunic and a grey cloak.

21
00:00:46,598 --> 00:00:48,933
At your belt hangs a small bronze case,

22
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:50,321
and inside it is the most

23
00:00:50,321 --> 00:00:53,324
important thing you own: a folding scale.

24
00:00:53,324 --> 00:00:55,108
Years ago, you left the fjords

25
00:00:55,108 --> 00:00:57,726
to work for a fur merchant in Birka,

26
00:00:57,726 --> 00:00:59,160
the Swedish trading town.

27
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:00,207
Your sister thinks

28
00:01:00,207 --> 00:01:02,093
you are doing very well.

29
00:01:02,093 --> 00:01:03,646
You have not corrected her.

30
00:01:03,646 --> 00:01:06,503
Popular culture sends the Vikings west,

31
00:01:06,503 --> 00:01:07,972
to England and Ireland.

32
00:01:07,972 --> 00:01:09,328
But Scandinavians,

33
00:01:09,328 --> 00:01:12,112
and especially Swedes, also travelled east,

34
00:01:12,112 --> 00:01:15,912
along the great rivers of what is now Russia and Ukraine.

35
00:01:15,912 --> 00:01:16,669
There,

36
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:19,768
they and their descendants became known as the Rus.

37
00:01:19,768 --> 00:01:22,514
This chapter follows one of their trade roads,

38
00:01:22,514 --> 00:01:23,795
from the Baltic all the way

39
00:01:23,795 --> 00:01:26,306
to the capital of the Byzantine Empire.

40
00:01:26,306 --> 00:01:27,896
And at the end of this story,

41
00:01:27,896 --> 00:01:31,082
we will read a painted stone on the island of Gotland,

42
00:01:31,082 --> 00:01:32,657
raised for a man who,

43
00:01:32,657 --> 00:01:35,734
it seems, never got past a rapid called Aifur.

44
00:01:36,264 --> 00:01:38,083
Your road east starts at Birka,

45
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:39,166
with furs:

46
00:01:39,166 --> 00:01:42,864
sable, squirrel and other skins from the northern forests.

47
00:01:43,302 --> 00:01:45,028
Light to carry, easy to pack,

48
00:01:45,028 --> 00:01:48,401
and worth a great deal of silver in the right market.

49
00:01:48,401 --> 00:01:51,484
A Persian geographer named Ibn Rustah,

50
00:01:51,484 --> 00:01:53,289
writing around the year nine hundred,

51
00:01:53,289 --> 00:01:56,415
described the Rus as people with no fields

52
00:01:56,415 --> 00:01:57,418
and no villages,

53
00:01:57,418 --> 00:02:01,406
whose only business was trading sable, squirrel and other furs.

54
00:02:01,406 --> 00:02:02,813
They kept the coins they

55
00:02:02,813 --> 00:02:04,629
were paid in their belts.

56
00:02:04,629 --> 00:02:07,308
He was describing strangers from a distance,

57
00:02:07,308 --> 00:02:09,296
and your master owns a house,

58
00:02:09,296 --> 00:02:11,992
two boats and an opinion about everything.

59
00:02:11,992 --> 00:02:13,401
But the furs were real,

60
00:02:13,401 --> 00:02:15,912
and so was the silver they brought.

61
00:02:15,912 --> 00:02:17,962
Ibn Rustah adds something darker.

62
00:02:18,449 --> 00:02:19,544
The Rus, he says,

63
00:02:19,544 --> 00:02:22,238
attacked the Slavs from their ships,

64
00:02:22,238 --> 00:02:24,292
took them captive, and sold them in the

65
00:02:24,292 --> 00:02:26,469
lands of the Khazars and the Bulgars.

66
00:02:26,469 --> 00:02:28,596
The National Museum of Denmark

67
00:02:28,596 --> 00:02:30,872
also names Bolghar, on the Volga,

68
00:02:30,872 --> 00:02:33,633
as a place where Vikings sold slaves.

69
00:02:34,024 --> 00:02:35,992
Your master's cargo is furs,

70
00:02:35,992 --> 00:02:37,428
honey and wax.

71
00:02:37,428 --> 00:02:38,792
Some of the other traders

72
00:02:38,792 --> 00:02:40,209
on the route carry people.

73
00:02:40,209 --> 00:02:41,968
You learn not to look at

74
00:02:41,968 --> 00:02:43,206
their boats for too long.

75
00:02:43,206 --> 00:02:45,436
You also learn languages, badly.

76
00:02:45,436 --> 00:02:48,972
A few words of Slavonic for food and water,

77
00:02:48,972 --> 00:02:51,038
a few words of Greek for numbers,

78
00:02:51,038 --> 00:02:52,264
and the one word

79
00:02:52,264 --> 00:02:55,518
every market on earth understands: no.

80
00:02:56,021 --> 00:02:58,810
What comes back up the rivers is silver.

81
00:02:59,233 --> 00:03:01,856
Hundreds of thousands of Islamic silver coins,

82
00:03:01,856 --> 00:03:04,156
called dirhams, have been found in

83
00:03:04,156 --> 00:03:06,821
Viking Age hoards across northern Europe.

84
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:08,801
The scholar Manar Hammad

85
00:03:08,801 --> 00:03:10,727
counts almost half a million

86
00:03:10,727 --> 00:03:13,116
dirhams in Scandinavian museums.

87
00:03:13,116 --> 00:03:15,904
On the Baltic island of Gotland,

88
00:03:15,904 --> 00:03:18,160
in nineteen ninety-nine, a find at a

89
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:20,022
farm called Spillings turned out to be

90
00:03:20,022 --> 00:03:21,597
one of the largest Viking silver

91
00:03:21,597 --> 00:03:23,173
hoards ever found:

92
00:03:23,173 --> 00:03:27,254
more than fourteen thousand coins, most of them dirhams.

93
00:03:27,254 --> 00:03:28,907
Somebody hid that silver

94
00:03:28,907 --> 00:03:30,608
and never came back for it.

95
00:03:30,608 --> 00:03:32,746
You intend to come back for yours.

96
00:03:33,184 --> 00:03:33,822
In spring,

97
00:03:33,822 --> 00:03:37,369
your master's boat leaves Birka and crosses the Baltic.

98
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:40,312
Historians reconstruct the road like this:

99
00:03:40,312 --> 00:03:43,299
up the river Neva into Lake Ladoga,

100
00:03:43,299 --> 00:03:45,911
up the Volkhov past Novgorod,

101
00:03:46,365 --> 00:03:47,782
then through a chain of smaller

102
00:03:47,782 --> 00:03:49,764
rivers and lakes towards the Dnieper.

103
00:03:50,459 --> 00:03:52,598
Where one river ends and the next

104
00:03:52,598 --> 00:03:55,586
has not yet begun, there is only land.

105
00:03:55,586 --> 00:03:56,994
So you unload,

106
00:03:56,994 --> 00:03:59,609
carry, drag and reload.

107
00:03:59,609 --> 00:04:02,528
These crossings are called portages,

108
00:04:02,982 --> 00:04:03,974
and each one is

109
00:04:03,974 --> 00:04:06,381
exactly as much fun as it sounds.

110
00:04:06,916 --> 00:04:09,874
Constantine, the emperor we are about to meet,

111
00:04:09,874 --> 00:04:12,068
also describes the Rus winter.

112
00:04:12,068 --> 00:04:13,644
In November, he says,

113
00:04:13,644 --> 00:04:16,440
their chiefs left Kiev with their men to spend

114
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:19,238
the winter among the Slavs who paid them tribute,

115
00:04:19,238 --> 00:04:20,497
and came back in April,

116
00:04:20,497 --> 00:04:22,805
when the ice on the river melted.

117
00:04:22,805 --> 00:04:24,989
He calls it a severe manner of life.

118
00:04:25,378 --> 00:04:26,327
You believe him.

119
00:04:26,327 --> 00:04:28,731
By the time you reach the Dnieper,

120
00:04:28,731 --> 00:04:30,545
your shoulders have opinions.

121
00:04:30,545 --> 00:04:32,375
Your master tells you

122
00:04:32,375 --> 00:04:33,561
this is the easy part.

123
00:04:33,561 --> 00:04:34,231
He is right,

124
00:04:34,231 --> 00:04:36,920
which is the worst news you have heard all year.

125
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:38,681
The best guide to what comes

126
00:04:38,681 --> 00:04:41,351
next was written by that emperor.

127
00:04:41,351 --> 00:04:42,511
Constantine the Seventh,

128
00:04:42,511 --> 00:04:45,188
ruler of the Byzantine Empire,

129
00:04:45,188 --> 00:04:47,748
wrote a handbook in Greek in the nine hundreds,

130
00:04:47,748 --> 00:04:50,630
and one chapter describes traders like you,

131
00:04:50,630 --> 00:04:52,596
coming down the river to his city.

132
00:04:53,131 --> 00:04:54,852
According to Constantine,

133
00:04:54,852 --> 00:04:57,964
Slavs cut boats called monoxyla in the winter,

134
00:04:57,964 --> 00:05:00,036
hollowed out of trees,

135
00:05:00,036 --> 00:05:02,272
and sold them to the Rus in the spring.

136
00:05:02,272 --> 00:05:04,451
The Rus bought only the hulls,

137
00:05:04,451 --> 00:05:05,965
and fitted them with oars,

138
00:05:05,965 --> 00:05:08,508
rowlocks and the rest of their tackle.

139
00:05:08,867 --> 00:05:11,049
So at Kiev, your master leaves his

140
00:05:11,049 --> 00:05:13,881
northern boat behind and buys a dugout,

141
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:17,850
a long, heavy hull made from a single great tree.

142
00:05:17,850 --> 00:05:19,778
You load it with furs,

143
00:05:19,778 --> 00:05:22,398
food, water, and two live chickens,

144
00:05:22,398 --> 00:05:25,119
for reasons he refuses to explain.

145
00:05:25,119 --> 00:05:27,704
Constantine says the boats came down

146
00:05:27,704 --> 00:05:29,216
to Kiev from Novgorod,

147
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:32,701
Smolensk and other towns, and in the month of June,

148
00:05:32,701 --> 00:05:35,405
they moved off down the Dnieper together.

149
00:05:35,405 --> 00:05:37,149
Kiev is crowded with boats,

150
00:05:37,149 --> 00:05:38,498
traders and arguments.

151
00:05:38,498 --> 00:05:39,678
Everyone has heard a

152
00:05:39,678 --> 00:05:41,921
story about the river below.

153
00:05:41,921 --> 00:05:44,092
Nobody agrees on the details,

154
00:05:44,092 --> 00:05:47,104
except one: you do not go alone.

155
00:05:47,104 --> 00:05:48,519
Why together?

156
00:05:48,958 --> 00:05:51,443
Because of what lies downstream.

157
00:05:51,443 --> 00:05:52,795
Below Kiev,

158
00:05:52,795 --> 00:05:55,943
the Dnieper drops through a long chain of rapids.

159
00:05:55,943 --> 00:05:58,269
Constantine names them one by one,

160
00:05:58,269 --> 00:06:01,490
in the language of the Rus and in Slavonic.

161
00:06:01,490 --> 00:06:03,248
The first, he says,

162
00:06:03,248 --> 00:06:06,599
is called Essoupi, which means: Do not sleep.

163
00:06:06,990 --> 00:06:08,162
Some modern scholars

164
00:06:08,162 --> 00:06:09,668
read the name differently.

165
00:06:09,668 --> 00:06:11,928
But as advice, it is excellent.

166
00:06:11,928 --> 00:06:13,664
At the rapids, you put in to

167
00:06:13,664 --> 00:06:15,596
the bank and unload the cargo.

168
00:06:15,596 --> 00:06:17,295
Then the men strip,

169
00:06:17,295 --> 00:06:19,786
climb into the water, and feel their way with

170
00:06:19,786 --> 00:06:22,097
their feet so as not to strike a rock,

171
00:06:22,097 --> 00:06:23,537
guiding the boat along,

172
00:06:23,537 --> 00:06:26,772
while others push from the stern with poles.

173
00:06:26,772 --> 00:06:28,451
The water is cold,

174
00:06:28,451 --> 00:06:32,063
the rocks are slippery, and the boat weighs more than you,

175
00:06:32,063 --> 00:06:35,083
your master and your ambitions combined.

176
00:06:35,083 --> 00:06:36,441
Nobody is singing.

177
00:06:36,441 --> 00:06:38,465
The worst is the great rapid,

178
00:06:38,465 --> 00:06:40,309
which the Rus called Aeifor.

179
00:06:40,309 --> 00:06:40,769
There,

180
00:06:40,769 --> 00:06:43,610
the boats come out of the water altogether.

181
00:06:43,610 --> 00:06:46,516
The crews drag them, or carry them on their shoulders,

182
00:06:46,516 --> 00:06:48,232
for about six miles.

183
00:06:48,232 --> 00:06:51,059
Six miles under a dugout is a long way.

184
00:06:51,448 --> 00:06:53,127
The hull presses into your neck,

185
00:06:53,127 --> 00:06:55,391
the ground is stony, and the man in front

186
00:06:55,391 --> 00:06:57,574
of you keeps stopping without warning.

187
00:06:57,574 --> 00:06:59,862
Your master walks beside the boat,

188
00:06:59,862 --> 00:07:00,983
carrying the chickens.

189
00:07:00,983 --> 00:07:02,940
Constantine adds one more detail,

190
00:07:03,250 --> 00:07:05,743
and it is the darkest line in his chapter.

191
00:07:05,743 --> 00:07:07,319
While the boats go round,

192
00:07:07,319 --> 00:07:09,008
the traders lead their slaves

193
00:07:09,008 --> 00:07:11,644
past the rapid on foot, in chains.

194
00:07:11,644 --> 00:07:13,115
And all the while,

195
00:07:13,115 --> 00:07:15,132
you watch the grasslands.

196
00:07:15,132 --> 00:07:18,353
Constantine says that it is at this

197
00:07:18,353 --> 00:07:19,731
point that the Pechenegs,

198
00:07:19,731 --> 00:07:23,073
the nomad riders of the steppe, come down and attack.

199
00:07:23,073 --> 00:07:25,134
That is why you travel in a fleet.

200
00:07:25,716 --> 00:07:28,047
A boat in the water is a ship.

201
00:07:28,047 --> 00:07:29,902
A boat on twenty men's

202
00:07:29,902 --> 00:07:31,668
shoulders is an invitation.

203
00:07:31,668 --> 00:07:33,293
Your fleet is lucky.

204
00:07:33,812 --> 00:07:35,688
A few riders watch from a ridge,

205
00:07:35,688 --> 00:07:37,266
count your spears,

206
00:07:37,266 --> 00:07:39,791
and decide to wait for someone smaller.

207
00:07:39,791 --> 00:07:42,012
You do not stop shaking until

208
00:07:42,012 --> 00:07:43,689
the boats are back in the water.

209
00:07:43,689 --> 00:07:45,968
Further down, the fleet reaches an

210
00:07:45,968 --> 00:07:48,476
island the Byzantines called Saint Gregory.

211
00:07:48,915 --> 00:07:52,144
Constantine says the Rus made sacrifices there,

212
00:07:52,144 --> 00:07:53,878
under a gigantic oak tree.

213
00:07:53,878 --> 00:07:56,077
They sacrificed live cocks,

214
00:07:56,077 --> 00:07:58,720
and cast lots to decide whether to kill the birds,

215
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:00,580
eat them, or let them live.

216
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:02,955
So that is what the chickens were for.

217
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:06,107
One of yours, by the lot, survives.

218
00:08:06,107 --> 00:08:08,066
It rides the rest of the way to the

219
00:08:08,066 --> 00:08:10,366
sea looking extremely pleased with itself.

220
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:12,204
You give it a name.

221
00:08:12,204 --> 00:08:13,594
This is a mistake.

222
00:08:13,594 --> 00:08:14,997
At last the river

223
00:08:14,997 --> 00:08:16,637
opens into the Black Sea.

224
00:08:16,637 --> 00:08:19,006
The crews rest for two or three days

225
00:08:19,006 --> 00:08:20,781
on an island near the river mouth,

226
00:08:20,781 --> 00:08:23,335
and fit their boats with masts and sails.

227
00:08:23,335 --> 00:08:25,630
Constantine sums up the journey

228
00:08:25,630 --> 00:08:28,436
so far as a voyage of travail and terror,

229
00:08:28,436 --> 00:08:30,192
difficulty and danger.

230
00:08:30,565 --> 00:08:31,918
He was not even on it.

231
00:08:31,918 --> 00:08:34,093
On the other great river road,

232
00:08:34,093 --> 00:08:36,221
the Volga, traders like you met

233
00:08:36,221 --> 00:08:38,870
a man who wrote everything down.

234
00:08:38,870 --> 00:08:39,490
In nine hundred and twenty-one,

235
00:08:39,490 --> 00:08:42,298
the caliph in Baghdad sent an embassy

236
00:08:42,298 --> 00:08:44,705
north to the king of the Volga Bulgars.

237
00:08:45,143 --> 00:08:48,369
Its secretary was Ahmad ibn Fadlan.

238
00:08:48,369 --> 00:08:49,749
On the banks of the Volga,

239
00:08:49,749 --> 00:08:52,298
he watched the Rus arrive with their goods.

240
00:08:52,608 --> 00:08:53,529
He had never seen

241
00:08:53,529 --> 00:08:54,858
people so tall, he wrote.

242
00:08:54,858 --> 00:08:56,820
They were as tall as palm trees,

243
00:08:56,820 --> 00:08:58,081
blond and ruddy.

244
00:08:58,081 --> 00:09:01,428
Every man carried an axe, a sword and a knife,

245
00:09:01,428 --> 00:09:03,553
and was never seen without them.

246
00:09:04,007 --> 00:09:04,951
He was much less

247
00:09:04,951 --> 00:09:06,506
impressed by their manners.

248
00:09:06,506 --> 00:09:07,562
He called them the

249
00:09:07,562 --> 00:09:09,854
filthiest of God's creatures.

250
00:09:09,854 --> 00:09:12,517
Ibn Fadlan was a man from Baghdad.

251
00:09:12,517 --> 00:09:12,978
To him,

252
00:09:12,978 --> 00:09:16,226
the Rus were strangers, and he wrote like it.

253
00:09:16,226 --> 00:09:18,125
He noticed their wealth, too.

254
00:09:18,125 --> 00:09:19,866
A Rus woman, he says,

255
00:09:20,224 --> 00:09:22,315
wore a neck ring for every ten thousand

256
00:09:22,315 --> 00:09:24,522
dirhams her husband was worth.

257
00:09:25,057 --> 00:09:26,816
And their most prized ornaments

258
00:09:26,816 --> 00:09:28,814
were beads of green glass,

259
00:09:28,814 --> 00:09:32,131
for which they paid a whole silver dirham each.

260
00:09:32,131 --> 00:09:34,214
Then he describes a scene

261
00:09:34,214 --> 00:09:35,874
every trader will recognise.

262
00:09:35,874 --> 00:09:38,096
When a Rus merchant arrived,

263
00:09:38,096 --> 00:09:40,150
he carried bread, meat, onions,

264
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:44,738
milk and drink to a tall wooden post carved with a human face,

265
00:09:44,738 --> 00:09:47,082
surrounded by smaller figures.

266
00:09:47,082 --> 00:09:49,256
He bowed down before it and

267
00:09:49,256 --> 00:09:51,263
listed what he had brought:

268
00:09:51,263 --> 00:09:55,181
so many slave girls, so many sable furs.

269
00:09:55,635 --> 00:09:57,125
Then came the request:

270
00:09:57,125 --> 00:10:00,186
send me a buyer with plenty of gold and silver,

271
00:10:00,186 --> 00:10:02,707
who buys whatever I want him to buy.

272
00:10:03,258 --> 00:10:04,727
If business was slow,

273
00:10:04,727 --> 00:10:08,632
Ibn Fadlan says, the merchant came back with a second offering,

274
00:10:08,632 --> 00:10:09,868
and then a third.

275
00:10:10,243 --> 00:10:12,257
If that still failed, he brought gifts to

276
00:10:12,257 --> 00:10:14,178
the smaller figures as well,

277
00:10:14,178 --> 00:10:16,759
and asked them to put in a good word with the big one.

278
00:10:17,165 --> 00:10:18,558
Selling has changed a

279
00:10:18,558 --> 00:10:20,584
great deal in a thousand years.

280
00:10:20,584 --> 00:10:21,968
The prayer has not.

281
00:10:21,968 --> 00:10:23,493
On your own river,

282
00:10:23,493 --> 00:10:27,270
your master makes no offerings to wooden posts.

283
00:10:27,270 --> 00:10:29,491
He simply mutters at the sky

284
00:10:29,491 --> 00:10:31,191
every time a buyer walks away.

285
00:10:31,191 --> 00:10:33,072
It seems to work about as well.

286
00:10:33,072 --> 00:10:34,507
Your own fleet hugs the

287
00:10:34,507 --> 00:10:36,243
coast of the Black Sea,

288
00:10:36,585 --> 00:10:39,328
and one morning, the skyline changes.

289
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:42,680
This is Miklagard, the Great City.

290
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:45,656
That is what the Norse called Constantinople,

291
00:10:45,656 --> 00:10:48,335
capital of the Byzantine Empire.

292
00:10:48,335 --> 00:10:50,123
On runestones back home,

293
00:10:50,123 --> 00:10:53,174
its whole empire is simply called Greece.

294
00:10:53,174 --> 00:10:55,877
You might expect a hero's welcome.

295
00:10:55,877 --> 00:10:56,876
You get paperwork.

296
00:10:56,876 --> 00:10:58,544
The city had its reasons.

297
00:10:58,999 --> 00:11:01,048
On the eighteenth of June,

298
00:11:01,048 --> 00:11:02,856
eight hundred and sixty, about two hundred Rus

299
00:11:02,856 --> 00:11:05,882
ships had appeared outside Constantinople.

300
00:11:06,208 --> 00:11:08,012
They could not take the city,

301
00:11:08,012 --> 00:11:09,155
and they withdrew.

302
00:11:09,155 --> 00:11:10,932
But Patriarch Photius,

303
00:11:10,932 --> 00:11:13,544
the head of the Church there, described the attack in

304
00:11:13,544 --> 00:11:15,977
a sermon as a terrible northern storm.

305
00:11:15,977 --> 00:11:18,341
Nobody in the city had forgotten.

306
00:11:18,827 --> 00:11:20,754
A chronicle kept later in Kiev,

307
00:11:20,754 --> 00:11:23,227
known as the Primary Chronicle,

308
00:11:23,227 --> 00:11:25,623
preserves treaties between the Rus and the

309
00:11:25,623 --> 00:11:27,539
emperors from the early nine hundreds.

310
00:11:28,059 --> 00:11:28,726
Under them,

311
00:11:28,726 --> 00:11:32,346
Rus merchants could enter the city through only one gate,

312
00:11:32,346 --> 00:11:35,084
unarmed, in groups of no more than fifty,

313
00:11:35,084 --> 00:11:37,783
escorted by an imperial official.

314
00:11:38,254 --> 00:11:39,568
They stayed near the

315
00:11:39,568 --> 00:11:40,946
church of Saint Mamas.

316
00:11:40,946 --> 00:11:43,200
Officials wrote down their names,

317
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:45,678
and only then could they receive their monthly supplies,

318
00:11:45,678 --> 00:11:47,329
for up to half a year.

319
00:11:47,329 --> 00:11:48,744
So you stand in line with

320
00:11:48,744 --> 00:11:50,119
forty-nine other northerners,

321
00:11:50,119 --> 00:11:51,934
no axe, no sword, no knife,

322
00:11:51,934 --> 00:11:55,423
while a clerk writes down a name he cannot pronounce.

323
00:11:55,423 --> 00:11:57,225
Behind him rise walls taller

324
00:11:57,225 --> 00:12:00,114
than any building you have ever seen.

325
00:12:00,114 --> 00:12:02,330
You try very hard to look like

326
00:12:02,330 --> 00:12:04,430
a merchant and not like a storm.

327
00:12:04,430 --> 00:12:06,620
Think about what those rules say.

328
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:09,339
The emperor's officials wanted the

329
00:12:09,339 --> 00:12:11,558
furs and the silver changing hands.

330
00:12:11,558 --> 00:12:13,372
They did not want fifty armed

331
00:12:13,372 --> 00:12:15,875
northerners wandering the city at once.

332
00:12:15,875 --> 00:12:18,878
Your master sells his furs in the market,

333
00:12:18,878 --> 00:12:20,688
and buys what the north wants:

334
00:12:20,688 --> 00:12:25,477
silk, wine, spices, glass and fine cloth.

335
00:12:25,915 --> 00:12:27,445
You buy something too:

336
00:12:27,445 --> 00:12:29,808
a string of green glass beads.

337
00:12:30,342 --> 00:12:32,766
Ibn Fadlan's Rus would have approved.

338
00:12:32,766 --> 00:12:34,974
Others left more than money behind.

339
00:12:34,974 --> 00:12:36,600
In the upper gallery of the

340
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:38,491
great church of Hagia Sophia,

341
00:12:38,491 --> 00:12:41,358
someone carved runes into the marble.

342
00:12:41,731 --> 00:12:43,322
Most of it is worn away,

343
00:12:43,322 --> 00:12:45,999
but scholars read the name Halfdan.

344
00:12:45,999 --> 00:12:48,365
Nobody knows exactly when he was there.

345
00:12:48,365 --> 00:12:49,419
Every tourist in

346
00:12:49,419 --> 00:12:51,392
history has had the same idea.

347
00:12:51,392 --> 00:12:52,092
Back home,

348
00:12:52,092 --> 00:12:54,756
your master's silver will not be counted.

349
00:12:54,756 --> 00:12:56,259
It will be weighed.

350
00:12:56,259 --> 00:12:58,629
Coins and ornaments are cut into pieces,

351
00:12:58,629 --> 00:13:01,027
tested with a knife and placed on a scale,

352
00:13:01,027 --> 00:13:02,650
just like the hacksilver your

353
00:13:02,650 --> 00:13:05,252
brother-in-law brought home from his raid.

354
00:13:05,252 --> 00:13:07,670
That is why you carry the folding scale.

355
00:13:08,301 --> 00:13:10,036
Traders of your time used small

356
00:13:10,036 --> 00:13:13,238
folding balances that packed away into metal cases,

357
00:13:13,238 --> 00:13:14,366
with standard weights,

358
00:13:14,366 --> 00:13:17,335
some shaped like cubes with their corners cut off,

359
00:13:17,335 --> 00:13:19,930
others round with flattened ends.

360
00:13:19,930 --> 00:13:22,174
The archaeologist Lotta Fernståhl notes

361
00:13:22,174 --> 00:13:24,758
that some of the round weights even carry

362
00:13:24,758 --> 00:13:26,855
imitation Arabic writing,

363
00:13:26,855 --> 00:13:30,497
copied from Islamic coins by people who could not read it.

364
00:13:31,016 --> 00:13:32,922
Even the jewellery travelled.

365
00:13:32,922 --> 00:13:36,428
At Birka, in a woman's grave from the eight hundreds,

366
00:13:36,428 --> 00:13:37,772
archaeologists found

367
00:13:37,772 --> 00:13:40,810
a silver ring set with a violet stone.

368
00:13:40,810 --> 00:13:42,478
For more than a century,

369
00:13:42,478 --> 00:13:45,451
it was catalogued as an amethyst.

370
00:13:45,451 --> 00:13:46,695
In twenty fifteen,

371
00:13:47,017 --> 00:13:50,502
researchers showed that the stone is coloured glass,

372
00:13:50,502 --> 00:13:53,280
and that the engraving on it is Arabic.

373
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:54,271
It reads:

374
00:13:54,710 --> 00:13:57,676
for Allah, or to Allah.

375
00:13:58,260 --> 00:13:59,808
The ring shows little wear,

376
00:13:59,808 --> 00:14:02,669
which suggests it passed through few hands

377
00:14:02,669 --> 00:14:04,763
between the silversmith and the grave.

378
00:14:04,763 --> 00:14:06,967
Who was the woman buried with it?

379
00:14:06,967 --> 00:14:08,326
A traveller herself,

380
00:14:08,326 --> 00:14:10,045
the wife of a trader,

381
00:14:10,045 --> 00:14:12,495
or simply someone who liked a beautiful ring?

382
00:14:13,013 --> 00:14:14,926
Researchers still debate it.

383
00:14:14,926 --> 00:14:16,810
The grave does not say.

384
00:14:17,120 --> 00:14:19,373
The road home is the same road in reverse,

385
00:14:19,373 --> 00:14:21,051
against the current.

386
00:14:21,051 --> 00:14:22,613
The rapids are no friendlier

387
00:14:22,613 --> 00:14:24,741
when you are carrying silver.

388
00:14:24,741 --> 00:14:26,889
You pass the island of Saint Gregory.

389
00:14:26,889 --> 00:14:29,101
The chicken does not come ashore.

390
00:14:29,101 --> 00:14:30,506
By the second autumn,

391
00:14:30,506 --> 00:14:32,375
you are back on the Baltic,

392
00:14:32,375 --> 00:14:35,928
salt-stained, thinner, and carrying a small leather

393
00:14:35,928 --> 00:14:38,444
bag of silver that is entirely yours.

394
00:14:38,444 --> 00:14:40,036
It is not a fortune.

395
00:14:40,036 --> 00:14:41,051
It is a cow,

396
00:14:41,051 --> 00:14:44,008
perhaps two, and a winter without worry.

397
00:14:44,008 --> 00:14:45,600
Your master counts his profit.

398
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:46,516
It is good.

399
00:14:46,874 --> 00:14:48,500
Then he subtracts the boats,

400
00:14:48,500 --> 00:14:50,126
the portages, the officials,

401
00:14:50,126 --> 00:14:53,003
the gifts, the chickens and a year of his life.

402
00:14:53,003 --> 00:14:55,222
It is still good, he decides.

403
00:14:55,222 --> 00:14:56,771
He decides this loudly.

404
00:14:56,771 --> 00:14:59,224
Not everyone came home from the east.

405
00:14:59,224 --> 00:15:01,769
On Gotland, in the museum at Visby,

406
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:04,758
stands a stone from the late nine hundreds.

407
00:15:04,758 --> 00:15:06,525
It was raised by a man named

408
00:15:06,525 --> 00:15:08,277
Hegbjorn and his brothers,

409
00:15:08,732 --> 00:15:10,922
in memory of a man named Hrafn.

410
00:15:10,922 --> 00:15:12,941
The inscription says that the

411
00:15:12,941 --> 00:15:14,589
stone was brightly painted,

412
00:15:14,589 --> 00:15:16,860
and that they came far into Aifur.

413
00:15:16,860 --> 00:15:20,221
Scholars connect Aifur with Aeifor,

414
00:15:20,221 --> 00:15:22,616
the great rapid named by Constantine,

415
00:15:22,616 --> 00:15:25,602
the one where the boats came out of the water.

416
00:15:25,602 --> 00:15:27,943
Hrafn's family could not bring him home.

417
00:15:28,430 --> 00:15:30,410
So they brought his name home instead,

418
00:15:30,410 --> 00:15:33,095
and painted it bright enough to be seen.

419
00:15:33,095 --> 00:15:34,797
He was not the only one.

420
00:15:34,797 --> 00:15:38,018
About thirty runestones in Sweden mention Greece,

421
00:15:38,018 --> 00:15:39,825
meaning the Byzantine Empire.

422
00:15:40,296 --> 00:15:41,703
Most remember men who

423
00:15:41,703 --> 00:15:43,174
died on their travels.

424
00:15:43,174 --> 00:15:45,015
A few simply say that

425
00:15:45,015 --> 00:15:46,261
a man had been there.

426
00:15:46,261 --> 00:15:48,318
So why was being a Viking trader on

427
00:15:48,318 --> 00:15:50,652
the eastern rivers worse than you think?

428
00:15:50,652 --> 00:15:52,521
Because the road to the

429
00:15:52,521 --> 00:15:54,018
silk was a road of rocks.

430
00:15:54,018 --> 00:15:56,269
You carried boats across land,

431
00:15:56,269 --> 00:15:58,976
waded through rapids named Do not sleep,

432
00:15:59,382 --> 00:16:01,139
watched the steppe for riders,

433
00:16:01,139 --> 00:16:04,522
and queued at a single gate with fifty unarmed men.

434
00:16:04,522 --> 00:16:07,566
And the silver you carried home was tied,

435
00:16:07,566 --> 00:16:09,826
more often than anyone liked to say,

436
00:16:09,826 --> 00:16:12,695
to people sold along the same rivers.

437
00:16:12,695 --> 00:16:14,639
And yet, that road linked the

438
00:16:14,639 --> 00:16:17,653
fjords to Constantinople and Baghdad.

439
00:16:17,653 --> 00:16:19,614
A ring engraved in Arabic

440
00:16:19,614 --> 00:16:21,578
ended up in a Swedish grave.

441
00:16:21,578 --> 00:16:23,704
Green glass beads ended

442
00:16:23,704 --> 00:16:24,957
up on a Norwegian farm.

443
00:16:24,957 --> 00:16:26,732
When you finally walk up the path

444
00:16:26,732 --> 00:16:28,594
to your sister's farm,

445
00:16:28,594 --> 00:16:31,532
Eirik is fixing the fence, Aslaug is at the loom,

446
00:16:31,532 --> 00:16:33,002
and a small girl is sitting

447
00:16:33,002 --> 00:16:35,374
in the grass, trying to eat a shoe.

448
00:16:35,374 --> 00:16:38,043
You give your niece a single green glass bead,

449
00:16:38,043 --> 00:16:39,541
for when she is older.

450
00:16:39,541 --> 00:16:42,123
Aslaug asks how rich you are now.

451
00:16:42,672 --> 00:16:43,954
You tell her the story

452
00:16:43,954 --> 00:16:45,459
about the chicken instead.

453
00:16:45,459 --> 00:16:46,521
Next spring,

454
00:16:46,521 --> 00:16:48,650
your master will talk about another voyage.

455
00:16:48,650 --> 00:16:50,633
You will pretend not to listen.

456
00:16:50,633 --> 00:16:52,132
And somewhere behind the house,

457
00:16:52,132 --> 00:16:53,791
the sheep has escaped again.

458
00:16:54,150 --> 00:16:55,566
This is Viking Chapters:

459
00:16:55,566 --> 00:16:57,566
the lives behind the legends.

460
00:16:57,940 --> 00:16:59,515
If you would like to see another

461
00:16:59,515 --> 00:17:00,708
life behind the legends,

462
00:17:00,708 --> 00:17:03,544
subscribe, and we will meet in the next chapter.
